# Your Verity gate runners

> **Global to YOU, across every project.** This is your private inventory of the
> machines that can EXECUTE a project's committed gate definition when its
> `.verity/autonomy.yml` says `gate_runner: remote:<name>` (ADR-0030). Project
> config references the NAME only — host details, users, and IPs stay here,
> outside every repo.
>
> ⚠️ **Reference credential LOCATIONS, never paste secrets.** Point at a key
> file *path* or an `~/.ssh/config` entry *name* — never an actual key,
> password, or token. This file lives at `~/.verity/gate-runners.md`; it is NOT
> in any repo, but treat it as sensitive anyway.
>
> Edit it any time with `verity gate-runners edit` (or open the path printed by
> `verity gate-runners path`). Add one `## <name> — <Title>` section per runner
> — names may use letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-` (exactly the charset
> `gate_runner: remote:<name>` accepts) — and set `- **status:** active` once
> it is real (the sample below is `example` — replace or delete it; an example
> entry never resolves as a real host). Each runner needs Docker (daemon
> running) and nektos/act installed; `verity doctor` probes both over your
> existing SSH context and never reads or logs credential contents.

## gpu-box — LAN GPU workstation (example — edit or delete)
- **status:** example
- **host:** `<hostname-or-ssh-config-alias>`
- **user:** `<remote-user>` — omit this line when the SSH-config alias carries it
- **access:** `ssh <user>@<host>` — key at `~/.ssh/<your-key>` (location only)
- **notes:** needs Docker + act installed; only reachable on the LAN / VPN.
